  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nSurface high will push across the state today, with a surface\nfront stalling near the ND/SD border. Cooler temperatures with\nthis high could bring some patchy to areas of frost across the\nnorthwest and north central this morning. Cooler temperatures\nwill then be found the rest of the day with highs near 60\ndegrees. Some breezy northwest winds are also possible today,\nmainly in the north and east. Along the mentioned stalled \nboundary could be some light rain showers, with slight chances \nmainly confined to the southwest. Surface high then moves\neastward tonight, bringing a southerly flow in its wake across\nthe west. Cooler temperatures will again be found with this high\ntonight, with widespread lows in the 30s. This will likely bring\nwidespread areas of frost across much of northern, central, and\neastern portions tonight into Friday morning. Local policy does\nstop issuance of frost highlights on October 1st, thus will hold\noff on any highlights at this time. Freeze highlights are still\npossible going forward if needed. NBM 10th percentile would \nhave temperatures near or slightly below freezing tonight. This \nwould require mainly clear skies and light winds. With the \nsurface high winds are expected to be light. However, the \nchanging flow aloft to west southwesterly looks to lift the \nstalled front and bring mid level clouds tonight. This could \nlimit overnight lows, thus left in the NBM forecast for now. \nPerhaps some light rain is also possible tonight from this \nlifting front, although with dry air at the surface left the \nforecast dry. This upper level front lifts across the state on \nFriday, while a surface gradient increases. The result will be \nslight chances for light rain and increased clouds on Friday. \nThe increased gradient will also bring some breezy southerly \nwinds. Look for slightly warmer temperatures Friday with highs \nin the 60s to lower 70s. A mainly dry surface low could then \nmove through Friday night, keeping temperatures more mild and in\nthe 40s.\n\nThis surface low and trailing cold front move through Saturday.\nLimited moisture with this system will provide for only slight\nchance of rain showers as this system moves through. Behind the\nmentioned front could be some breezy northwest winds. Look for\nhighs mainly in the 60s during the day Saturday, with lows back\nin the mid 30s to lower 40s Saturday night. Surface high with\nridging aloft then looks to move through on Sunday. This will\nbring light winds, mainly dry conditions, and near to slightly\nbelow normal temperatures. Cool nighttime lows could again be\nfound Sunday night, with lows in the mid 30s to mid 40s.\nClusters indicate ridging to perhaps a flat ridge (zonal flow)\npattern could be found to start next week. The result is a\nreturn to above normal temperatures with mainly dry conditions.\nA more zonal flow pattern could allow weak waves to move through\nat times early to mid next week, although the overall chances\nfor precipitation is low. An increased surface gradient on\nMonday could also bring some breezy southerly winds. Overnight\nlows early to mid next week also look to be mild and generally\nabove freezing.",
